meta {
  name: Create practice
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/practice
  body: multipartForm
  auth: none
}

body:multipart-form {
  attendace-list: @file(fixtures/attendance-2026-01-15.xlsx)
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("redirects to the new practice", function() {
    expect(res.getHeader("hx-redirect")).to.match(/^\/practices\/\d+$/);
  });
}

docs {
  Multipart upload — the endpoint that's hardest to feed bad data through
  the browser form.

  Put a real MyClub attendance export at
  `bruno/fixtures/attendance-2026-01-15.xlsx` first. That folder is
  gitignored, since exports contain real names.

  Two things to know before you start:

  - The FILENAME matters as much as the contents. `file.ParseDate` reads
    the practice date out of it.
  - The form field is `attendace-list` — note the typo. It's spelled that
    way in both create_practice.templ and the handler, so it has to match
    here too.

  Success is `200`, empty body, `HX-Redirect` to the new practice.

  ## Edge cases

  | Change | Expected |
  |---|---|
  | disable the file field | `200`, EMPTY body — nothing happens |
  | rename the field to `attendance-list` (correct spelling) | same silent `200` |
  | send `fixtures/not-an-excel.txt` | `500`, empty body |
  | send a valid xlsx named with no date | `500`, empty body |
  | header `Cookie: user_session_id=nope` | `401` + `HX-Redirect: /login` |

  Fixtures to create for those:

      echo "not a spreadsheet" > bruno/fixtures/not-an-excel.txt
      cp <a real export> bruno/fixtures/no-date-in-name.xlsx

  ## What each case does

  **No file.** `r.FormFile` fails, the handler logs and returns without
  writing a status, so Go defaults to `200 OK` with an empty body. HTMX
  swaps the target with nothing and the page doesn't move.

  **Correctly-spelled field name.** Hits the same path as no file at all,
  since the handler looks for `attendace-list` and doesn't find it.

  **Non-Excel file.** `accept=".xlsx"` only filters the browser's file
  picker; it isn't enforced server-side, so the upload reaches `excelize`,
  which can't open it.

  ## Ordering

  `handleCreatePractice` parses the file, looks up every player, and
  distributes the teams BEFORE it parses the date from the filename. On
  the bad-filename case all of that work happens and is then discarded.
  Nothing is written to the DB — the transaction starts after the date
  parse.
}
